00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.160
The phrase that we’re going to be hearing&nbsp;
a lot as we cover the subject is “passive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.380
income,” that’s the thing that’s being promised, a&nbsp;
source of income that requires little or no active&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:49.020
upkeep in order to maintain, like dividends,&nbsp;
residuals, and royalties. You put in a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.060
thousand hours writing a book or making a&nbsp;
movie and then once it’s out there you keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.060 --> 00:02:00.000
making money as long as people keep buying it, and&nbsp;
getting people to buy it is someone else’s job.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.140
The concept in and of itself is not&nbsp;
fraudulent, it is in fact a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.640
sweet deal and that’s why we should support&nbsp;
universal basic income as a political policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.280
but passive income in a rhetorical&nbsp;
context becomes a red flag because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.840
how it’s deployed as a very effective&nbsp;
lure into fraudulent businesses.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.100
Contrepreneurs are a dime a dozen, there’s&nbsp;
mountains of these people out there, because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:32.520
as we’ll explore, it’s pretty cheap and it’s&nbsp;
reasonably legal. We’ll get into more detail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:37.500
