00:24:15.180 --> 00:24:19.920
buying the premium AIA package gets you&nbsp;
access to things like discount codes.

00:24:19.920 --> 00:24:24.060
“we've worked out an exclusive&nbsp;
deal with our favorite ghostwriting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:28.320
company to provide you with the lowest&nbsp;
ghostwriting prices humanly possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.320 --> 00:24:33.000
it's the same deal normally only we can&nbsp;
get because we're friends with the CEO”

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:37.440
Following all of Christian’s advice,&nbsp;
minus the options that no longer exist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.440 --> 00:24:39.480
I picked the cheapest package available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.860 --> 00:24:44.880
25,000 words, and let The Urban Writers&nbsp;
make as many decisions for me as possible.

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:46.560
So, what did I give them?

00:24:46.560 --> 00:24:46.830
Well.

00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:52.980
I wanted to test the ethical limits of the system&nbsp;
by presenting something that was just ever so over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.980 --> 00:24:57.900
the line, because the hitch in this is that once&nbsp;
you submit your pitch to The Urban Writers there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.900 --> 00:25:03.900
a whole back end where the writers basically&nbsp;
compete to take posted projects. Obviously if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:03.900 --> 00:25:08.700
cooked up something very deliberately offensive&nbsp;
there’s a good chance that no one would take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:08.700 --> 00:25:13.560
it on as a project, and I wouldn’t consider&nbsp;
that to be an ethical success of the system.

00:25:13.560 --> 00:25:18.540
And on the off-chance someone behind the scenes&nbsp;
did take the job, that’s either because they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.540 --> 00:25:24.360
so desperate that they’re willing to write&nbsp;
hate speech or they’re just already comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.360 --> 00:25:29.820
writing hate speech, and I didn’t want to&nbsp;
be party to either of those possibilities.

00:25:29.820 --> 00:25:35.700
So let’s keep it at least a little bit&nbsp;
lighter: what if we make some just truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.700 --> 00:25:41.160
irresponsible health claims and use that as&nbsp;
the basis to create a book that’s not hateful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:44.520
but still really shouldn’t exist?

00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:50.700
Can self-hypnosis treat epilepsy?&nbsp;
Can it maybe even cure it?

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:52.500
We don’t have the answer to that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.500 --> 00:25:58.200
but we have a lot of leading questions that’ll&nbsp;
let you come to that conclusion on your own in

00:25:58.200 --> 00:26:03.960
Treating Epilepsy with Self-Hypnosis: A Guide&nbsp;
to a Free and Healthy You by Brad Default

00:26:03.960 --> 00:26:07.920
I cooked up the title, came up with a pen&nbsp;
name, threw together an outline that felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:12.900
like it would at least be able to hit&nbsp;
25,000 words, mashed in Keto dieting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:17.760
and something about prions just to cover&nbsp;
my bases, tag-wise, and submitted it.

00:26:17.760 --> 00:26:22.200
Now, the Urban Writers as an outfit is&nbsp;
worth some scrutiny in their own right.

00:26:22.200 --> 00:26:26.100
The Urban Writers is the creation&nbsp;
of Marco and Natasha Moutinho,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.100 --> 00:26:30.480
a husband and wife team of&nbsp;
Canadian… entrepreneurs.

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:36.240
“what's up everyone I'm Marco and&nbsp;
I'm Natasha and we're the Moutinhos”

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:40.260
The Urban Writers was founded in&nbsp;
2017 as a division of Dibbly Inc.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:40.260 --> 00:26:43.560
alongside Dibbly Publishing, both&nbsp;
also creations of the Moutinhos.

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:47.640
No, I don’t know why their own name&nbsp;
is misspelled on their own website.

00:26:47.640 --> 00:26:52.080
The Urban Writers manages to look mostly&nbsp;
legit at first blush and if you don’t know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:52.080 --> 00:26:55.980
anything about the industry of ghost&nbsp;
writing then it all sounds… normal?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:56.700 --> 00:27:00.900
The Urban Writers functions as a job-posting&nbsp;
board for ghostwriting contracts.

00:27:00.900 --> 00:27:04.860
In the Mikkelsen presentation it is postured&nbsp;
as though there’s the option to just,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.860 --> 00:27:09.300
like, submit something and walk&nbsp;
away, let TUW assign a writer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:12.420
and then just come back and pick up&nbsp;
the deliverables when it’s all done.

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:14.520
Maybe that was the case in the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:14.520 --> 00:27:18.600
but that was not accurate to my&nbsp;
experience and I suspect I know why.

00:27:18.600 --> 00:27:23.160
See, the critical word in there&nbsp;
was “assign.” If The Urban Writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:28.500
are assigning projects to specific writers&nbsp;
then that’s not a contractor relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:34.080
that person is an employee. So, and&nbsp;
you probably already guessed this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:34.080 --> 00:27:41.220
The Urban Writers is, in fact, a post-Uber gig&nbsp;
economy nightmare where “””freelancers””” are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:44.700
treated like employees to the maximum&nbsp;
extent the owners can get away with.

00:27:44.700 --> 00:27:49.080
“What makes us unique is the culture&nbsp;
we have fostered within our community.”

00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:50.340
Yeah, sure.

00:27:50.340 --> 00:27:56.580
So my pitch gets picked up by Scott. Scott is&nbsp;
not his real name, because writers at TUW are not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:56.580 --> 00:28:02.100
permitted to use anything other than a pen name,&nbsp;
and are subject to disciplinary action if they use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.100 --> 00:28:06.600
their real names, disclose their real names,&nbsp;
or provide a means of being contacted outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:06.600 --> 00:28:12.540
the platform. They’re also not free to negotiate&nbsp;
their own rates, but we’ll get back to that later.

00:28:12.540 --> 00:28:17.460
Scott is a champion. Scott took all&nbsp;
Brad’s insane ideas about prions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:22.260
keto and self hypnosis and epilepsy and&nbsp;
very delicately tied them together into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:22.260 --> 00:28:27.360
a somewhat morally defensible product. Just&nbsp;
to really drive this point, we like Scott.

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:31.740
The end result is not good, it’s more&nbsp;
or less exactly what you would expect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:36.060
but it exists and was delivered on time,&nbsp;
and that alone is something of a miracle.

00:28:36.060 --> 00:28:38.400
Before I get into some of&nbsp;
the details about the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:43.380
I want to talk a bit about those expectations,&nbsp;
and kinda broadly about ghostwriting itself.

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:49.860
I do not mean for this to be, in any way, an&nbsp;
impugnment of ghostwriting as a craft. It’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:49.860 --> 00:28:55.500
niche of writing that requires professionalism&nbsp;
and a set of specific skills. Ghostwriters need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:55.500 --> 00:29:00.000
to be able to adopt the voice of their subject&nbsp;
and typically need to work under particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:05.760
rigorous delivery timelines, something not&nbsp;
all writers can do. So a bunch of the flaws&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:09.780
and failings that we’re going to go into&nbsp;
are not strictly the result of ghostwriting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.780 --> 00:29:14.100
in and of itself, but are the product of&nbsp;
the overall environment that is curated.

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:18.180
When I say “it’s exactly what you would&nbsp;
expect” I mean that if you know a thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.180 --> 00:29:23.700
or two about writing long form nonfiction then&nbsp;
you can probably look at the expectations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:29.160
draw a few natural conclusions about what the&nbsp;
end product more or less needs to look like.

00:29:29.160 --> 00:29:35.400
The deliverable is 25,000 words, the turnaround&nbsp;
is 30 days with a few days in there for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:35.400 --> 00:29:42.180
administrative junk, so basically 25 days of&nbsp;
actual writing, average of 1000 words per day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:48.180
that’s a pretty stiff pace but not an absurd&nbsp;
requirement of a professional. The big hitch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:53.940
there is research. The main thing that slows down&nbsp;
a nonfiction writer is the need to look stuff up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:53.940 --> 00:29:58.740
source quotes, and fact-check claims.&nbsp;
Depending on the subject it might take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:58.740 --> 00:30:03.540
a really long time just to find a couple&nbsp;
useful sources, and often the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.540 --> 00:30:08.400
you discover a source is garbage is by&nbsp;
spending hours and hours processing it.

